### What is the Max Consecutive Ones problem?

Given a binary array containing only 0s and 1s, find the length of the longest contiguous subarray of all 1s. This is one of the simplest linear scan problems and a natural introduction to the sliding window family.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MaxConsecutiveOnes {
    public static int findMaxConsecutiveOnes(int[] nums) {
        int current = 0;
        int best = 0;
        for (int num : nums) {
            if (num == 1) {
                current++;
                if (current > best) best = current;
            } else {
                current = 0;
            }
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] settlementDays = {1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1};
        System.out.println("Longest success streak: " + findMaxConsecutiveOnes(settlementDays));
    }
}
```

```text
Longest success streak: 3
```

### Why does the count-and-reset approach work?

A run of ones is only ever extended by seeing another one immediately after, and it is only broken by a zero. The current counter exactly measures the length of the run ending at the present position, and taking the maximum over all positions gives the longest run anywhere in the array.

```java
// Walkthrough: [1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1]
// Index 0: num=1, current=1, best=1
// Index 1: num=1, current=2, best=2
// Index 2: num=0, current=0
// Index 3: num=1, current=1
// Index 4: num=1, current=2
// Index 5: num=1, current=3, best=3
// Index 6: num=0, current=0
// Index 7: num=1, current=1
// Final best: 3
```

### Why update best on every one instead of on every zero?

Updating best right after incrementing on each 1 is always correct. Some people update best only when they hit a 0, but then they must remember to also update once more after the loop in case the array ends in a run of ones. Updating on every 1 avoids that end-of-array special case entirely.

```java
// WRONG: update only on 0, forgetting end-of-array
for (int num : nums) {
    if (num == 1) current++;
    else {
        best = Math.max(best, current); // updates here
        current = 0;
    }
}
// BUG: if array ends with ones, best may not include the last streak
// Fix: best = Math.max(best, current); after the loop

// BETTER: update on every 1, no post-loop fix needed
if (num == 1) {
    current++;
    if (current > best) best = current;
}
```

### How does this generalize to the K-flip version?

The Max Consecutive Ones III problem allows flipping at most K zeros to ones and asks for the longest run. That is solved with a sliding window that counts zeros in the window. The plain problem is the K=0 special case.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MaxConsecutiveOnesIII {
    public static int longestOnes(int[] nums, int k) {
        int left = 0, zeros = 0, best = 0;
        for (int right = 0; right < nums.length; right++) {
            if (nums[right] == 0) zeros++;
            while (zeros > k) {
                if (nums[left] == 0) zeros--;
                left++;
            }
            best = Math.max(best, right - left + 1);
        }
        return best;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] days = {1, 1, 0, 1, 1, 1, 0, 1};
        System.out.println("With 1 flip: " + longestOnes(days, 1)); // 6
        System.out.println("With 0 flips: " + longestOnes(days, 0)); // 3 (same as plain)
    }
}
```

```text
With 1 flip: 6
With 0 flips: 3
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty array has no ones, so the answer is zero. An array of all ones returns the full length. An array of all zeros returns zero. A single-element array returns 1 if it is a 1, or 0 if it is a 0.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class MaxConsecutiveEdgeCases {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(MaxConsecutiveOnes.findMaxConsecutiveOnes(new int[]{}));     // 0
        System.out.println(MaxConsecutiveOnes.findMaxConsecutiveOnes(new int[]{1,1,1})); // 3
        System.out.println(MaxConsecutiveOnes.findMaxConsecutiveOnes(new int[]{0,0,0})); // 0
        System.out.println(MaxConsecutiveOnes.findMaxConsecutiveOnes(new int[]{1}));     // 1
        System.out.println(MaxConsecutiveOnes.findMaxConsecutiveOnes(new int[]{0}));     // 0
    }
}
```

```text
0
3
0
1
0
```

### What about input validation?

The array is expected to contain only 0s and 1s. In a defensive implementation you might validate each element, but for an interview problem the contract is trusted. For a boolean-valued stream you could use a boolean array or a BitSet and the same counting logic applies.

```java
// Defensive version:
for (int num : nums) {
    if (num != 0 && num != 1) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Not binary: " + num);
    // ... same counting logic
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to update best after the loop when updating only on zeros, which misses a run that extends to the end of the array.

```java
// WRONG: misses trailing run
int current = 0, best = 0;
for (int num : nums) {
    if (num == 1) current++;
    else { best = Math.max(best, current); current = 0; }
}
// Missing: best = Math.max(best, current);
// Input [1,1,1] returns best=0 instead of 3

// CORRECT: update on every 1
if (num == 1) { current++; best = Math.max(best, current); }
```

### When should you use this technique?

Use the count-and-reset pattern for any longest-streak problem on a sequence with a binary condition. In banking, this applies to computing reliability metrics like the longest streak of successful settlement days or the longest period without a fraud alert.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class SettlementReliability {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] monthlySettlements = {1,1,1,0,1,1,1,1,1,0,1,1};
        int streak = MaxConsecutiveOnes.findMaxConsecutiveOnes(monthlySettlements);
        System.out.println("Longest monthly success streak: " + streak + " months");
    }
}
```

```text
Longest monthly success streak: 5 months
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer should recognize that this is the K=0 case of the sliding-window Max Consecutive Ones III problem. Mentioning the generalization shows depth in an interview. The early-termination optimization (if best + remaining <= best, stop) is rarely worth the check overhead for this simple scan.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

Interviewers commonly ask:
- What if you can flip at most K zeros? (Sliding window counting zeros.)
- What if the input is a stream, not an array? (Same count-and-reset, no random access needed.)
- Can you do it without branching? (current = current * num + num, best = max(best, current).)
- Time and space? (O(n) time, O(1) space.)
